WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.243 --> 00:00:01.195
(airplane whooshes)

00:00:01.195 --> 00:00:03.247
(basketball bounces)

00:00:03.247 --> 00:00:04.701
- What's up Believe Nation it's Evan.

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:06.889
My one word is believe and I believe

00:00:06.889 --> 00:00:09.586
that you have the ability
to create something special

00:00:09.586 --> 00:00:11.089
that will change the planet.

00:00:11.089 --> 00:00:12.449
So to help you on your journey

00:00:12.449 --> 00:00:14.008
I started the Mentor Me series.

00:00:14.008 --> 00:00:16.125
And the goal here is to
try to hang around people

00:00:16.125 --> 00:00:17.587
who've done a lot more than us,

00:00:17.587 --> 00:00:19.594
who've achieved massive success,

00:00:19.594 --> 00:00:22.274
and hopefully by hanging
around them a little bit longer

00:00:22.274 --> 00:00:24.747
some of their mindsets, their
attitudes, their beliefs,

00:00:24.747 --> 00:00:27.367
the way they see the
world will seep into us

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:30.241
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:30.241 --> 00:00:32.712
So today we're going to
learn from Malcolm Gladwell

00:00:32.712 --> 00:00:35.567
and some of his best advice
from David and Goliath.

00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:36.665
Mentor me Malcolm.

00:00:36.665 --> 00:00:40.832
(inspirational instrumental music)

00:00:49.898 --> 00:00:50.977
- I arrived at the idea

00:00:50.977 --> 00:00:54.495
for the book because I had done this piece

00:00:54.495 --> 00:00:56.045
for The New Yorker some years ago

00:00:56.045 --> 00:01:00.212
called How David Beats Goliath
about a guy out in California

00:01:01.073 --> 00:01:05.240
who coached his daughter's
12-year-old basketball team

00:01:07.111 --> 00:01:09.481
all the way to the National Championships.

00:01:09.481 --> 00:01:13.194
Even though they were a
group entirely without talent

00:01:13.194 --> 00:01:17.177
or skill, and he did
it by, for those of you

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:20.061
who are basketball fans, by
playing the full court press

00:01:20.061 --> 00:01:22.917
every minute of every game.

00:01:22.917 --> 00:01:24.362
A really, really, really radical form

00:01:24.362 --> 00:01:28.052
of the full court press, the
most aggressive form you can.

00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:32.248
And I just thought that
story was hilarious,

00:01:32.248 --> 00:01:35.308
but also really interesting
because I loved the way

00:01:35.308 --> 00:01:37.891
in which he refused to give up.

00:01:41.339 --> 00:01:43.301
So here was, he was a guy who knew nothing

00:01:43.301 --> 00:01:45.106
about basketball, coaching a team of girls

00:01:45.106 --> 00:01:47.090
without any obvious skills in the area.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.675
And the normal response of
people in those situations

00:01:51.675 --> 00:01:53.547
is either to say it's pointless,

00:01:53.547 --> 00:01:55.557
and lose by 30 points a game.

00:01:55.557 --> 00:01:59.822
Or to try desperately to play
by the rules of everyone else,

00:01:59.822 --> 00:02:01.798
like to play the way
everyone else is playing,

00:02:01.798 --> 00:02:05.199
and try and catch up which
would be impossible in a season.

00:02:05.199 --> 00:02:09.734
And he chose option C
which was to hell with it.

00:02:09.734 --> 00:02:13.988
Let's play in this way that's
so deeply subversive that,

00:02:13.988 --> 00:02:17.856
you know, the other team isn't
going to know what hit them.

00:02:17.856 --> 00:02:21.190
And that story just stayed with me

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:22.240
and I thought it would be really fun

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.969
to write a book about
those kinds of strategies.

00:02:24.969 --> 00:02:26.281
So there's this really interesting fact

00:02:26.281 --> 00:02:29.265
that a very large percentage,
a much larger percentage

00:02:29.265 --> 00:02:32.352
of successful entrepreneurs are dyslexic

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:35.360
than in the general population.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.193
And many of the Richard
Branson, Paul Orfalea,

00:02:40.274 --> 00:02:43.433
Charles Schwab, John Chambers at Cisco.

00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:45.880
I could go on, Craig McCaw,
the cellphone pioneer.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.647
The list of these guys are all dyslexic.

00:02:48.647 --> 00:02:51.701
Right? David Neeleman at JetBlue.

00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:53.342
And if you talk to them
they will explain to you

00:02:53.342 --> 00:02:54.975
that they don't think they succeeded

00:02:54.975 --> 00:02:55.939
in spite of their disability,

00:02:55.939 --> 00:02:59.432
they think they'd succeeded because of it.

00:02:59.432 --> 00:03:00.934
For them, and if you want,

00:03:00.934 --> 00:03:05.086
I sat down with two dozen of these guys.

00:03:05.086 --> 00:03:06.773
I got sort of obsessed at the beginning

00:03:06.773 --> 00:03:08.438
of my book, in the middle of my book

00:03:08.438 --> 00:03:11.621
with talking to dyslexic entrepreneurs,

00:03:11.621 --> 00:03:13.938
and their stories are all the same.

00:03:13.938 --> 00:03:16.120
They all look back and will tell you,

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.242
"You know if it hadn't
have been for the fact

00:03:18.242 --> 00:03:22.515
"that I couldn't read or read
well in second, and third,

00:03:22.515 --> 00:03:25.124
"and fourth grade I would never have,"

00:03:25.124 --> 00:03:26.626
And they start listing all
the things they were forced

00:03:26.626 --> 00:03:29.529
to do that proved to be
ultimately advantageous.

00:03:29.529 --> 00:03:31.845
"I would never have
learned how to listen."

00:03:31.845 --> 00:03:32.901
"I would never have been forced,

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:35.995
"in second grade I made
friends with the smartest kid

00:03:35.995 --> 00:03:37.923
"in the class, and I
basically convinced him

00:03:37.923 --> 00:03:39.801
"to do my homework for me."

00:03:39.801 --> 00:03:40.832
I can't tell you how many times I heard

00:03:40.832 --> 00:03:41.665
that from these people.

00:03:41.665 --> 00:03:42.886
So what are they learning at that age?

00:03:42.886 --> 00:03:44.980
They're learning delegation,
they're learning how

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:47.425
to communicate with other people,

00:03:47.425 --> 00:03:50.342
motivate other people, form a team.

00:03:52.342 --> 00:03:55.473
Brian Grazer, the Hollywood
producer who's dyslexic,

00:03:55.473 --> 00:03:58.223
his whole thing was he would fail

00:04:00.705 --> 00:04:01.810
his tests, and he would go

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:05.336
in and he would talk his
grade up from a D to a C.

00:04:05.336 --> 00:04:07.694
So from the age of this high,

00:04:07.694 --> 00:04:10.144
he's learning negotiation, right?

00:04:10.144 --> 00:04:13.168
By the end by the time he hits
college he's brilliant at it.

00:04:13.168 --> 00:04:14.001
And then what did he do?

00:04:14.001 --> 00:04:16.738
He becomes a Hollywood
producer. What is that about?

00:04:16.738 --> 00:04:18.886
It's about negotiation among other things.

00:04:18.886 --> 00:04:21.468
And he's been practicing his entire life.

00:04:21.468 --> 00:04:24.141
So there's this weird
thing where he would say,

00:04:24.141 --> 00:04:26.974
"As difficult as my dyslexia was,"

00:04:27.983 --> 00:04:31.699
and for all of these people
they're childhoods were not fun.

00:04:31.699 --> 00:04:34.179
I mean, I interviewed Gary Cohn

00:04:34.179 --> 00:04:37.673
who's the president of Goldman,
who's profoundly dyslexic.

00:04:37.673 --> 00:04:41.840
And his childhood just sounds,
I mean dark and miserable.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.986
No one thought he was
capable of doing schoolwork.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:48.847
They thought he was, they were amazed

00:04:48.847 --> 00:04:51.907
that he could graduate from high school.

00:04:51.907 --> 00:04:53.868
Despite that they all
look back and they say,

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:57.634
"You know, it was a desirable difficulty.

00:04:57.634 --> 00:04:59.306
"I was forced to learn stuff

00:04:59.306 --> 00:05:01.640
"I would never even have thought about."

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.606
In order to learn the things
that really need to be learned,

00:05:04.606 --> 00:05:07.173
we require a certain level of adversity.

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:09.217
The trick is figuring out what

00:05:09.217 --> 00:05:11.527
that adversity ought to look like, right?

00:05:11.527 --> 00:05:12.803
And that's like I said something

00:05:12.803 --> 00:05:16.614
that can only be decided
on a case by case basis.

00:05:16.614 --> 00:05:18.843
It's going to be different
for you than it is for me.

00:05:18.843 --> 00:05:21.085
It's not enough to have a
great idea, and the focus,

00:05:21.085 --> 00:05:23.784
and the conscientiousness
to see it to fruition.

00:05:23.784 --> 00:05:26.512
You must have the
strength, and the resolve,

00:05:26.512 --> 00:05:30.332
and the courage to pursue
that idea even when the rest

00:05:30.332 --> 00:05:33.441
of the world thinks you're insane, right?

00:05:33.441 --> 00:05:36.267
Time and time again if
you look at the stories

00:05:36.267 --> 00:05:39.808
of extraordinarily
important entrepreneurs,

00:05:39.808 --> 00:05:44.314
there is almost always a moment
when they are the only ones

00:05:44.314 --> 00:05:46.775
who believe in the value
of what they're doing.

00:05:46.775 --> 00:05:49.178
You know, I tell in my book, my book

00:05:49.178 --> 00:05:51.770
David and Goliath, the
story of Ingvar Kamprad,

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.581
the guy who founds IKEA.

00:05:53.581 --> 00:05:56.423
And the crucial moment in the story

00:05:56.423 --> 00:05:59.593
of IKEA is when he faces a boycott

00:05:59.593 --> 00:06:02.298
from the other furniture
manufactures in Sweden

00:06:02.298 --> 00:06:04.106
and he's about to go out of business.

00:06:04.106 --> 00:06:07.752
And in desperation he moves his operations

00:06:07.752 --> 00:06:09.634
across the Baltic Sea from Sweden

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:12.677
to Poland and sets up shop in Poland.

00:06:12.677 --> 00:06:14.315
And that's what IKEA is.

00:06:14.315 --> 00:06:18.029
IKEA is essentially furniture
shipped flat made in Poland.

00:06:18.029 --> 00:06:21.072
That's the original
elevator pitch for IKEA.

00:06:21.072 --> 00:06:24.484
What's interesting about
that is he does it in 1961

00:06:24.484 --> 00:06:26.354
at the height of the Cold War.

00:06:26.354 --> 00:06:28.758
At a time when East and
West, communist world

00:06:28.758 --> 00:06:31.628
and free world are closer to outright war

00:06:31.628 --> 00:06:35.235
than at any other time in
history, a guy living in the West,

00:06:35.235 --> 00:06:38.066
Sweden, crosses the pond to Poland,

00:06:38.066 --> 00:06:40.421
the Iron Curtain and sets up shop.

00:06:40.421 --> 00:06:44.682
You can not imagine what a
controversial move that was.

00:06:44.682 --> 00:06:47.684
That would be like
Walmart opening operations

00:06:47.684 --> 00:06:50.252
in North Korea, literally,
it's on that level

00:06:50.252 --> 00:06:53.826
of kind of eyebrow raising,
"you've got to be kidding me,

00:06:53.826 --> 00:06:56.087
"who is this guy," kind of thing.

00:06:56.087 --> 00:06:57.823
But he does it, and he persists,

00:06:57.823 --> 00:07:00.243
and he turns his back
on all those critics.

00:07:00.243 --> 00:07:04.448
Why? Because he is a
deeply disagreeable person.

00:07:04.448 --> 00:07:06.823
Didn't need people to
agree with him, right?

00:07:06.823 --> 00:07:08.728
And that's how he's able to build IKEA

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:12.188
into this extraordinary
runaway success story.

00:07:12.188 --> 00:07:16.355
That's very hard to do, as
human beings we are hardwired

00:07:17.456 --> 00:07:19.471
to want the approval of our peers.

00:07:19.471 --> 00:07:22.134
I retell the story of David
and Goliath to start the book.

00:07:22.134 --> 00:07:24.086
Because if you look at the story closely

00:07:24.086 --> 00:07:26.294
everything you think was
a disadvantage about David

00:07:26.294 --> 00:07:28.998
actually isn't and Goliath
is not what he looks like.

00:07:28.998 --> 00:07:32.431
He looks like this
indomitable giant, in fact,

00:07:32.431 --> 00:07:35.345
he probably had a medical
condition called acromegaly

00:07:35.345 --> 00:07:38.538
which is, it's just sort of a side thing.

00:07:38.538 --> 00:07:40.213
- You're bringing medicine
into the Bible dude.

00:07:40.213 --> 00:07:41.229
What are you? (laughs)

00:07:41.229 --> 00:07:44.353
- It is hilarious because
the minute you start digging

00:07:44.353 --> 00:07:46.521
into these things you
discovery these little pockets

00:07:46.521 --> 00:07:48.332
have been arguing about
these things for years.

00:07:48.332 --> 00:07:50.968
For 50 years endocrinologists
have been arguing

00:07:50.968 --> 00:07:55.135
about whether Goliath had a
tumor on his pituitary gland

00:07:57.032 --> 00:07:58.354
which would explain is height.

00:07:58.354 --> 00:08:00.731
Because when you have a
tumor on your pituitary gland

00:08:00.731 --> 00:08:02.743
you overproduce human growth hormone.

00:08:02.743 --> 00:08:04.056
- [Host] Right.

00:08:04.056 --> 00:08:06.425
- Andre the Giant had this condition.

00:08:06.425 --> 00:08:07.429
- Seven-foot-four, a beast.

00:08:07.429 --> 00:08:08.374
- Seven-foot-four.
- Yeah.

00:08:08.374 --> 00:08:09.830
- But one of the side effects is

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:12.146
that it can constrict your optic nerves

00:08:12.146 --> 00:08:15.874
and leave you largely with
very, very limited eyesight.

00:08:15.874 --> 00:08:19.232
So one of the explanations
for why Goliath behaved

00:08:19.232 --> 00:08:22.485
so strangely and why he doesn't perceive

00:08:22.485 --> 00:08:25.304
that David is not intending to fight him.

00:08:25.304 --> 00:08:27.132
I mean, David comes down the mountain,

00:08:27.132 --> 00:08:28.878
and he's got no sword and no armor.

00:08:28.878 --> 00:08:31.135
He clearly is not intending
to fight a sword fight,

00:08:31.135 --> 00:08:34.238
and Goliath just sits
there like he's oblivious.

00:08:34.238 --> 00:08:38.089
Well, the answer is, maybe he
can only see this far, right?

00:08:38.089 --> 00:08:40.251
So it changes everything when
you realize, wait a minute,

00:08:40.251 --> 00:08:42.293
the giant is weighed down by 100 pounds

00:08:42.293 --> 00:08:45.182
of armor and can't see anything.

00:08:45.182 --> 00:08:47.616
He's like, like this, right?

00:08:47.616 --> 00:08:49.628
So, anyway, I do more with that story.

00:08:49.628 --> 00:08:51.633
But it's all about this,
trying to get people

00:08:51.633 --> 00:08:55.800
to take a step back and
understand that we can give up

00:08:57.356 --> 00:09:01.523
or feel powerless because we
have these empty definitions,

00:09:02.759 --> 00:09:05.549
or flawed definitions of advantage.

00:09:05.549 --> 00:09:07.741
If you've read, you know,
Innovator's Dilemma,

00:09:07.741 --> 00:09:10.124
that's what Innovator's
Dilemma is all about, right?

00:09:10.124 --> 00:09:14.396
The disruptive outsider is
the one who is incapable

00:09:14.396 --> 00:09:16.795
of meeting the marketplace needs

00:09:16.795 --> 00:09:19.054
as the market is traditionally defined.

00:09:19.054 --> 00:09:21.627
They can't do it, right,
so what do they do?

00:09:21.627 --> 00:09:25.245
They try a completely
new half-assed approach

00:09:25.245 --> 00:09:27.630
which in the beginning
doesn't work, right?

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:28.609
But by that very nature

00:09:28.609 --> 00:09:31.040
of trying something completely
outside the mainstream

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.746
they end up upending the ...

00:09:33.746 --> 00:09:37.610
Were they any good they would
never be forced to do that.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.173
So it's the same kind of principle.

00:09:39.173 --> 00:09:42.002
- Nobody who is talking about your book

00:09:42.002 --> 00:09:44.389
in the mainstream media
is mentioning the element.

00:09:44.389 --> 00:09:45.554
You say that there are three things,

00:09:45.554 --> 00:09:47.074
it's faith, courage, and determination.

00:09:47.074 --> 00:09:47.924
- [Malcolm] Yeah.

00:09:47.924 --> 00:09:49.975
- Nobody's talking about faith.

00:09:49.975 --> 00:09:54.629
- It's an odd thing, I
mean, this book began as,

00:09:54.629 --> 00:09:57.458
I just wanted in the
manner of my previous books

00:09:57.458 --> 00:10:00.251
to do this kind of, use
a lot of social science,

00:10:00.251 --> 00:10:02.564
work through theories, tell some stories

00:10:02.564 --> 00:10:05.053
about the fact that disadvantages

00:10:05.053 --> 00:10:08.230
are often advantageous,
and advantages can be.

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:09.626
But by the end of the book I realized

00:10:09.626 --> 00:10:11.914
that what I really wanted
to talk about was faith,

00:10:11.914 --> 00:10:14.119
was about the weapons of the spirit.

00:10:14.119 --> 00:10:15.277
I have a chapter where I talk

00:10:15.277 --> 00:10:19.082
about this little town in
the mountains of France.

00:10:19.082 --> 00:10:20.920
During this, it was a bunch of huguenots,

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.148
a dissident Protestant sect

00:10:23.148 --> 00:10:25.623
in a very Catholic country
up in the mountains.

00:10:25.623 --> 00:10:26.672
During the Second World War

00:10:26.672 --> 00:10:30.024
they decided they were going to harbor

00:10:30.024 --> 00:10:32.062
any Jewish refugees
that came to their door.

00:10:32.062 --> 00:10:35.082
And the Jewish refugees
came by the thousands.

00:10:35.082 --> 00:10:38.644
They took them in and they told the Nazis,

00:10:38.644 --> 00:10:40.646
"We're taking them in, if you want to come

00:10:40.646 --> 00:10:42.463
"and get them we're not
going to give them to you.

00:10:42.463 --> 00:10:44.424
"We don't care what you do to us."

00:10:44.424 --> 00:10:45.570
It's this extraordinary story,

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:48.367
and if you read into it they had nothing.

00:10:48.367 --> 00:10:51.566
They had no resources, no weapons.

00:10:51.566 --> 00:10:55.400
They refused to lie, so
they weren't using deceit.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:59.075
All they had was their
faith, and that was enough.

00:10:59.075 --> 00:11:01.778
They felt that their faith
was every bit the match

00:11:01.778 --> 00:11:04.085
for whatever the Nazis threw at them.

00:11:04.085 --> 00:11:07.272
And you can not, I don't
care if you are the most dyed

00:11:07.272 --> 00:11:10.772
in the wool atheist, you
can not read that story

00:11:10.772 --> 00:11:14.522
and not come away with
a renewed appreciation

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.703
for the power that faith gives people.

00:11:18.703 --> 00:11:19.536
(airplane whooshes)

00:11:19.536 --> 00:11:21.463
- Thank you guys so much for
watching, I hope you enjoyed.

00:11:21.463 --> 00:11:23.781
I'd love to know what did
you learn from this video

00:11:23.781 --> 00:11:26.444
that you're going to
immediately apply somehow

00:11:26.444 --> 00:11:27.956
to your life or your business.

00:11:27.956 --> 00:11:30.019
What was the single most
important lesson that you learned?

00:11:30.019 --> 00:11:31.511
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:11:31.511 --> 00:11:32.681
I'm really curious to find out.

00:11:32.681 --> 00:11:34.978
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Dave Gardner.

00:11:34.978 --> 00:11:37.258
Dave, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:11:37.258 --> 00:11:40.178
Your One Word, and for doing
that YouTube review on it.

00:11:40.178 --> 00:11:42.264
I really, really, really
appreciate the support.

00:11:42.264 --> 00:11:44.210
And I'm so glad that you enjoyed the book.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:45.461
- I'm doing a little review

00:11:45.461 --> 00:11:48.282
of Your One Word which
is by Evan Carmichael.

00:11:48.282 --> 00:11:49.833
- So thank you guys again for watching.

00:11:49.833 --> 00:11:51.658
I believe in you, and I hope you continue

00:11:51.658 --> 00:11:55.013
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:11:55.013 --> 00:11:57.445
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:11:57.445 --> 00:11:58.990
(airplane whooshes)

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:00.256
(basketball bounces)

